24827:
Bringing q_3 from infinity costs q_3 times the potential set up by q_1 and q_2 at the empty vertex
V = \frac{k (q_1 + q_2)}{a} = \frac{9 \times 10^9 \times 3 \times 10^{-9}}{0.03} = 900 \text{ V}
W = q_3 V = 3 \times 10^{-9} \times 900 = 2.7 \times 10^{-6} \text{ J} = 2.7 \mu\text{J} → (D)

24828:
Cells in series drive I_s = \frac{2 E}{R + 2 r} through R = 6 \Omega
Cells in parallel drive I_p = \frac{E}{R + r / 2} = \frac{2 E}{2 R + r}
Equal currents need R + 2 r = 2 R + r, giving r = R = 6 \Omega → (B)

24830:
Parallel currents attract with force per length \frac{\mu_0 I_1 I_2}{2 \pi d}
Wire Q feels opposite pulls from P and R, and the stronger nearer wire wins
Net force on the 15 \text{ cm} length is 6 \times 10^{-6} \text{ N} towards R → (A)

24832:
Gravity pulls down the slope with m g \sin 30^{\circ} = 5 \times 10 \times 0.5 = 25 \text{ N}
Friction resists up the slope with \mu m g \cos 30^{\circ} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} \times 50 \times \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} = 25 \text{ N}
Gravity exactly balances friction, so uniform sliding needs no extra push → (C)

24834:
Balancing length follows terminal voltage V = E \frac{R}{R + r}
\frac{4 / (4 + r)}{12 / (12 + r)} = \frac{120}{180} = \frac{2}{3}
Cross-multiplying gives 12 + r = 8 + 2 r, so r = 4 \Omega → (D)

24836:
Warming the ice costs 10 \times 2100 \times 10 = 2.1 \times 10^5 \text{ J} and melting costs 10 \times 3.36 \times 10^5 = 33.6 \times 10^5 \text{ J}
Heat lost by the bulk water feeds the ice and its melt: 100 \times 4200 \times (25 - T_f) = 35.7 \times 10^5 + 10 \times 4200 \times T_f
This gives T_f = 15^{\circ}\text{C}, a drop of 25 - 15 = 10^{\circ}\text{C} → (D)

24837:
Plano-concave power magnitude is |P_1| = \frac{1.7 - 1}{R_2} = \frac{0.7}{R_2}
Biconvex power magnitude is |P_2| = (1.5 - 1) (\frac{1}{R_1} + \frac{1}{R_2}) = 0.5 (\frac{1}{R_1} + \frac{1}{R_2})
Equating the magnitudes and solving for the radii gives \frac{R_1}{R_2} = \frac{5}{12}
Front to back radii stand in ratio 5 : 12 → (B)

24838:
Same load and same stretch give Y = \frac{F L}{A \Delta L} for each wire
\frac{Y_A}{Y_B} = \frac{L_A}{L_B} \times \frac{A_B}{A_A} = \frac{6.0}{5.4} \times \frac{4.5}{3.0} = \frac{5}{3}
\frac{5}{3} = \frac{x}{3} gives x = 5 → (D)

24839:
With jaws closed the 4th division coincides, so zero error is +4 \times 0.1 = +0.4 \text{ mm}
Observed length is 15 + 5 \times 0.1 = 15.5 \text{ mm}
True length = 15.5 - 0.4 = 15.1 \text{ mm} → (D)

24841:
Axial field scales as B = B_0 \frac{R^3}{(R^2 + x^2)^{3/2}}
At x^2 = 3 R^2 the denominator is (4 R^2)^{3/2} = 8 R^3
B = \frac{16}{8} = 2 \mu\text{T} → (C)

24842:
Mean square current is I_{rms}^2 = \frac{1}{T} \int_0^T i_0^2 \frac{t^2}{T^2} d t
Integration gives \frac{i_0^2}{T^3} \times \frac{T^3}{3} = \frac{i_0^2}{3}
I_{rms} = \frac{i_0}{\sqrt{3}} → (C)

24843:
The first drop covers 5 \text{ m} in t = \sqrt{2 h / g} = \sqrt{2 \times 5 / 10} = 1.0 \text{ s}
Five equal gaps make \Delta t = 0.2 \text{ s}, so the 4th drop has fallen for 0.4 \text{ s}
It has fallen \frac{1}{2} g (0.4)^2 = 0.8 \text{ m}, leaving 5 - 0.8 = 4.2 \text{ m} → (B)

24845:
Cyclic work is the area trapped between the parabola (V - 2)^2 = 4 a p and the straight paths
Integrating the enclosed strip gives W = \frac{1}{3 a}
Net work \frac{1}{3 a} → (C)

24847:
Lensmaker in air reads \frac{1}{f} = (1.5 - 1) (\frac{1}{R_1} - \frac{1}{R_2})
Under water the factor drops to \frac{1.5}{4 / 3} - 1 = \frac{9}{8} - 1 = \frac{1}{8}, one quarter of \frac{1}{2}
Focal length grows to f_w = 4 f = 72 \text{ cm}, and the rise 72 - 18 = 54 \text{ cm} = 3 f gives \alpha = 3 → (3)

24849:
Potential energy is largest at full stretch x = A
A \sin(\omega t) = A needs \omega t = \frac{\pi}{2}, hence t = \frac{T}{4}
\frac{T}{4} = \frac{T}{2 \beta} gives \beta = 2 → (2)

24850:
De Broglie wavelength is \lambda = \frac{h}{\sqrt{2 m K}}
\frac{\lambda_d}{\lambda_{\alpha}} = \sqrt{\frac{4 m \times 2 E}{2 m \times E}} = \sqrt{4} = 2
Ratio 2 : 1 fixes n = 2 → (2)
